ractive than  the places where there is shooting  is already perfectly disgusting  unfortunately  everyone does not seem to be possessed  of patience the corps and army  commanders  see that the others in other parts have  obtained tangible successes  so they thirst for laurels too it is a  human  weakness but they think it is a virtue  not to lag behind the others  and do not realize that this virtue must  be paid for  in blood cold storms  snowflakes that make the horses shake  their heads with annoyance  warn us that we must get ready for cold  weather today for the first time it is  quieter on the front  the trenches filled with icy water do  not encourage one to reinforce the  slender garrisons with which both friend  and foe are holding the fire  both parties are in the same situation  the business may last for a long time  we have now been stuck in the same spot  for five weeks  for five weeks the newspapers have been  saying that epri has to be the decision  of this phase of the war  at least this may have been right a few  weeks ago  but the decision depends also on the  time taken to affect it  now this part of the front is losing the  character of a decisive sector  what we have gained in ground and the  enemy's losses however great  can only be called a success by a man  who does not see both sides  four apart from the fact that our losses  have probably balanced those of the  enemy  no one can possibly estimate at present  what this breathing space of five weeks  may mean for france and england  we may be stuck for good and all  now the losses are often the fault of  the men how men do not trouble their  heads over the fact that only picked  snipers are firing from the enemy lines  men who at a ranger often less than 100  yards cannot help hitting every head  that shows itself  it is not contempt of death that makes  them expose themselves to certain death  it is a lack of discipline precise  instructions and orders on the subject  have been drawn up bu